  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Gorges du Caramy stretch for about 4.5 km, from the Saut du Cabri (Mazaugues) to the Pont de Cassède, called the "Roman bridge" in Tourves but actually dating from the 15th century and listed as a Historic Monument since 1937. The Provence Verte & Verdon tourist office describes walking, swimming, fishing and picnics there.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Dogs must be kept on a leash in the gorges, according to the Sainte-Baume regional park's hiking sheet. An important point confirmed by Tourves town hall and the tourist office: in summer, from 15 June to 15 September, all vehicles are banned from driving up to the main car park (about 40 spaces); you need to park at the P1 or P2 car parks near the stadium, then walk about 2 km (30 minutes). In June-September, access to the massif is also regulated by the prefecture depending on fire risk: check the evening map before setting out.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: before heading off, stop by the Musée des Gueules Rouges in Tourves, which traces bauxite mining in the Var.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>In the area, keep your dog on a leash everywhere. Warning: Lac de Carcès, often cited in the region, is a drinking-water reservoir where swimming is banned, so don't plan on it for your dog.</p>
